WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.120
- In July, the Federal Trade Commission

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.870
fined Facbook for $5 billion
for violating users' privacy.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.660
This is the largest fine
the FTC has ever levied

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.560
against a business.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.840
In 2012, the commission sued Facebook

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.230
for misleading consumers

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.950
about third-party access to user data,

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.500
and more recently, the
FTC found that Facebook

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.440
was again sharing data
with third-party companies

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.500
even though many consumers had requested

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.810
that their data remained private.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.380
The settlement puts Facebook
under more intense scrutiny

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.420
from the commission.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.050
Here's how the FTC says
their rules for Facebook

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.950
could protect consumers.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.610
Change number one, third-party apps

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.910
will find it harder to get at your data.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.920
The settlement will force
Facebook to increase

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.220
its management of third-party developers.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.520
Facebook must now
terminate developer access

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.560
to user information if that developer

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.853
can't justify their need for that data.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.230
Change number two, Facebook
will need your active consent

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.213
to keep your facial-recognition data.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.240
The FTC writes that
the company must delete

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.310
any existing facial-recognition data

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.310
within 90 days of the order

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.360
unless the company obtains
users' affirmative consent

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.760
to use it on templates in the future.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.000
Change number three, Facebook
can't use your phone number

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.720
for its advertising business
without your consent.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.970
Has Facebook ever requested

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.760
that you add a phone
number for added security?

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.453
It turns out Facebook may
have used it to help sell ads.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.840
The new FTC order mandates
that any given number

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.600
in the guise of enhanced security

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.783
may not be shared with advertisers
or other third parties.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.940
Change number four, Facebook
must quickly notify authorities

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.180
when data has been compromised.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.470
The order also mandates that
Facebook increase transparency

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.390
when data of 500 or more
users has been compromised.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.690
Facebook has to deliver
notice within 30 days

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.870
of the company's discovery of the incident

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.420
alongside a description of steps

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.003
taken to remediate the issue.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.850
The order will not resolve every issue

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.140
that consumers may have with Facebook.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.960
In a press hearing,
multiple FTC representatives

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.180
stressed that changes may
not be immediately obvious.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.420
- It is not the purpose
of this investigation

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.360
to vindicate every concern

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.610
that the world has about Facebook.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.360
We have big national
conversations going on

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.230
about the company and
others like it right now.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:19.820
Those are important conversations.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.490
They're going to continue.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.010
- Some at the FTC say the
restrictions don't go far enough.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.530
One commissioner said that the settlement

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.970
imposes no meaningful
changes on the systems

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.533
that led to these violations.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.440
The FTC order is narrowly
focused on Facebook

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.280
and more limited in scope than Europe's

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.040
General Data Protection
Regulation, or GDPR.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.210
That's because the U.S.
doesn't have a comprehensive

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.510
privacy protection law, FTC officials say.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.610
For that reason, FTC officials
say they can't do much

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.610
to limit the kinds of data
Facebook's collecting.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.710
Instead, they have to focus on making sure

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.200
that Facebook doesn't
continue misleading consumers

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.310
about how their data is being used.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.660
In a press release, Facebook
said that the settlement

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.367
will, quote, "mark a
sharper turn toward privacy,

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:06.329
"on a different scale than
anything we've done in the past."

00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:10.829
(pleasant piano and orchestral music)

